Microcephaly
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Head circumference <2 SD below mean, Sloping forehead, Global developmental delay, Reduced cranial volume on imaging

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-031
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of intractable seizures and severe feeding intolerance causing suspected aspiration.
Location: Group residential home
Present at Visit: Patient and primary support worker
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a 3-hour history of escalating, focal motor seizures without return to baseline, accompanied by continuous gagging and regurgitation of feeds. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the imminent risk of non-convulsive status epilepticus and fatal airway obstruction secondary to severe dysphagia, requiring emergent in-home medical stabilization.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He has been twitching continuously on his right side and violently coughing up his formula since dinner.
History of Present Illness: The support worker reports an acute deterioration in a 14-year-old with a known occipitofrontal head circumference measuring more than two standard deviations below the mean for age and sex. The patient, who has severe global developmental delay and spastic quadriplegia, developed continuous focal twitching of the right upper extremity 3 hours ago. Concurrently, poor feeding and failure to thrive have worsened into total feeding intolerance today, with the patient regurgitating all enteral fluids and exhibiting a wet, gurgling cough. The patient's baseline hyperactivity is currently replaced by marked lethargy.
Functional Status: Fully dependent, bedbound, non-verbal.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of aspiration pneumonia and prolonged seizure-induced brain injury.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 92% (Room Air) | Wt: 35.0 Kg
General: Patient is unresponsive to verbal stimuli, exhibiting continuous rhythmic twitching of the right arm and pooling secretions in the oropharynx.
Physical Exam: Cranial exam reveals a sloping forehead with a disproportionately normal-sized face, and a markedly reduced cranial volume on imaging history. Neurological exam confirms ongoing focal clonic seizure activity and severe generalized hypertonia (spastic quadriplegia). Ocular exam shows known visual impairments with searching nystagmus. Respiratory auscultation reveals transmitted upper airway rhonchi and decreased air entry at the right lung base.
Environmental Observations: Continuous enteral feeding pump was left running despite the regurgitation, exacerbating aspiration risk.
Medication Reconciliation: Routine anticonvulsants administered this morning; PRN buccal midazolam available but not given by staff due to fear of worsening the choking.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Microcephaly (complicated by focal status epilepticus and acute aspiration) - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of a high fever or nuchal rigidity, and the direct correlation of the respiratory symptoms to a witnessed feeding event.

Status Asthmaticus - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of expiratory wheeze and the presence of continuous focal neurological seizure activity driving the clinical picture.

Simple Febrile Seizure - Ruled out/unlikely given the patient's age (14 years), the focal nature of the seizure, and the duration exceeding 3 hours.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Life-threatening emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in focal status epilepticus complicated by acute gastric aspiration and hypoxia (SpO2 92%). The objective findings of continuous clonic twitching, pooled oropharyngeal secretions, and basal lung crackles in a patient with profound structural brain malformations demanded immediate intervention. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because prolonged status epilepticus causes irreversible anoxic injury, and the mechanical airway compromise required immediate suctioning and pharmacological seizure termination to prevent respiratory arrest.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered buccal Midazolam 10mg immediately, which successfully terminated the focal seizure activity within 4 minutes. Positioned the patient in the lateral recovery posture and performed deep oropharyngeal suctioning to clear pooled formula. Ceased all enteral feeding.
Positive Behavioral Change: When staff rigidly enforce a 45-degree upright position during and for one hour after every tube feed, gravity prevents the formula from refluxing into the lungs; therefore, adhering to this strict positioning protocol prevents life-threatening aspiration events. When you immediately pause the feeding pump at the first sign of coughing, you limit the volume of inhaled fluid; making this a mandatory reflex protects the patient's airway.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the complex care pediatric team for gastrostomy tube assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure functional suction equipment is kept continuously at the bedside.
Follow-up: Immediate transfer via 000 for inpatient management of aspiration pneumonia and adjustment of baseline anticonvulsants.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently being transported to the Emergency Department via ambulance under my direct supervision.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The underlying structural brain deficit remains unchanged; treatment targets the acute complications of the profound neurological impairment.
Clinical Course: The patient will require IV antibiotics for the aspiration pneumonia and a formal swallow study before enteral feeding can be safely resumed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Ensure all mechanical suction devices are emptied, cleaned, and tested at the start of every shift. Never leave a feeding pump running if the patient is coughing or seizing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: His damaged brain entered a continuous seizure loop, which relaxed his airway muscles and allowed the feeding formula to spill directly into his lungs. The emergency medication stopped the seizure, and suctioning cleared the upper airway, but the formula already in his lungs requires hospital treatment with IV antibiotics.
Actionable Steps: Keep the feeding pump turned off. Prepare his medical summary for the paramedics. Follow all hospital team instructions regarding future feeding methods.

